in @glass

# Property: a pre-commit gate's check set must equal (or be bounded by)
# the checks whose dependency closure intersects the commit diff.
#
# Recognition #43 (mirror IS a content-addressed build system; candidate
# — surfaced 2026-06-10) names mirror's substrate-altitude possession
# of every Bazel/Buck2/Nix/Shake primitive: dependency closure,
# content-addressed cache, partial-rebuild verdict. Recognition #51
# (mirror as expanding Hilbert space; promoted) places the pre-commit
# gate at the path-syntax altitude where each check is one coordinate
# of the cascade. Recognition #50 (Bateson form/substance; promoted)
# places the gate's CHECK-SET DECISION on the form side — substrate
# discipline; the BYTE-LEVEL TEST EXECUTION is substance-side @io work
# under Taut's territory (@io/cargo, @mirror/spec).
#
# The substrate already had the words: `mosaic` (the substrate's build
# shard), `closure` (the dependency-resolution carrier), `diff` (the
# substrate's mutation carrier). This property generalises the discipline
# the substrate was already practising implicitly for `partial(opacity_map)`
# rebuild verdicts — but at the pre-commit gate altitude rather than the
# materialise altitude.
#
# Concrete vs parametric: the predicate inspects concrete pre-commit
# gate declarations only — a gate is a named set of checks (cargo test,
# mirror parse, formatter, etc.) declared at the gate altitude. The
# gate itself is parametric over commit; the property checks each
# concrete (gate, commit) pairing.
#
# Verdict surface is transparency<p>:
#   success              — every check in the gate's run set has a
#                          dependency closure that intersects the
#                          commit diff (no over-running) AND every
#                          check whose closure intersects the diff is
#                          in the gate's run set (no under-running).
#   failure(opacity_map) — the gate runs at least one check whose
#                          closure does NOT intersect the diff
#                          (over-running; the friction Reed surfaced
#                          across this session — cargo test on a pure-
#                          .mirror commit), or skips at least one
#                          check whose closure DOES intersect the
#                          diff (under-running; a Rust-touching commit
#                          that skipped cargo test would be the dual).
#   partial(opacity_map) — multiple gate-commit pairings, some match
#                          and some don't (per-pulse mixed verdict).
#
# Located transparency (not @glass.verdict): the kintsugi loop consumes
# the opacity_map to direct mutation. Each opacity names ONE offending
# (check, commit) pairing; the fracture body
# (@kintsugi/fracture/gate; sibling-spawn tick) lifts violations through
# kintsugi. The property names the rule; the fracture realises it; the
# kintsugi loop is the bridge.
#
# Bilateral with @epistemologic/pact/keyword_matches_depth (the
# first instance of the bilateral pattern, candidate recognition #53):
# keyword_matches_depth constrains WHICH KEYWORD a declaration uses;
# gate_matches_diff_closure constrains WHICH CHECKS a gate runs. Same
# bilateral shape: declarative property at @epistemologic/property/<X>
# + operational fracture body at @kintsugi/fracture/<X>, bridged by
# the kintsugi loop's active_pass reading typed transparency.
#
# This is the SECOND INSTANCE of bilateral pattern #53. Two instances
# under one shape = the pattern promotes from candidate to recognition.
#
# Cybernetic ancestor: recognition #37 (`requires` IS a Paskian
# agreement, promoted 2026-06-10) names `pact` as the property-
# declaration form. pact replaces the conversation-era `grammar`
# keyword as the surface for declaring properties; the rule names
# `pact` directly below.
#
# Build-systems ancestor: every modern build system (Bazel, Buck2,
# Nix, Shake, Pants, Turbo) names this property implicitly as the
# CORRECTNESS PROPERTY of incremental rebuilds — only run what the
# diff invalidates. The substrate names it explicitly so the
# kintsugi loop can READ the violation and PROPOSE the fix declaratively.
# Per [[architecture-mirror-as-content-addressed-build-system]]: the
# substrate had every primitive; this property names the discipline.

pact @epistemologic/pact/gate_matches_diff_closure {
  # The set of checks the gate declares it WILL run (its declared
  # run set). One entry per check name; entries are gate-altitude
  # refs to the check declaration.
  gate_run_set(gate: ref) -> [ref] { \ }

  # The set of checks whose dependency closure intersects the
  # commit diff. Each check declares its dependency closure
  # (cargo test depends on *.rs + Cargo.toml; mirror parse depends
  # on *.mirror; formatter depends on the file's altitude). The
  # diff is the substrate's mutation carrier per @kintsugi/morphism.
  # This output is the substrate-derived ground truth the gate's
  # run set is measured against.
  diff_closure_intersect(gate: ref, commit: ref) -> [ref] { \ }

  # The combined property: every check in `gate_run_set` is in
  # `diff_closure_intersect` (no over-running) AND every check in
  # `diff_closure_intersect` is in `gate_run_set` (no under-running).
  # Verdict surface is transparency<p>:
  #   success              — the two sets are equal.
  #   failure(opacity_map) — at least one check is in exactly one
  #                          of the two sets; each opacity names
  #                          ONE such (check, commit) pairing and
  #                          flags whether it's over-running
  #                          (in gate_run_set, not in closure) or
  #                          under-running (in closure, not in
  #                          gate_run_set) via opacity.property.
  #   partial(opacity_map) — multiple (gate, commit) pairings, some
  #                          match and some don't.
  gate_matches_diff_closure(gate: ref, commit: ref) -> transparency { \ }
}

out gate_run_set
out diff_closure_intersect
out gate_matches_diff_closure
out @epistemologic/pact/gate_matches_diff_closure
